<?phem

namespace App\Ui\Demos;

use Phem\Attribute\Page;
use Phem\Node;

use function App\Ui\{DemoPage, Example, Examples, Hero};
use function Phem\Ui\Base\Examples\{TooltipAnimatedDemo, TooltipControlledDemo, TooltipDetachedDemo, TooltipHeroDemo, TooltipMultipleDemo};

#[Page('/components/tooltip')]
function TooltipPage(): Node
{
    return (
        <DemoPage slug="tooltip">
            <Hero source="TooltipHeroDemo">
                <TooltipHeroDemo />
            </Hero>
            <Examples>
                <Example title="Detached triggers" source="TooltipDetachedDemo">
                    <Example.description>
                        <p>A tooltip can be controlled by a trigger located either inside or outside the <code>Tooltip</code> component. For simple, one-off interactions, place the <code>TooltipTrigger</code> inside <code>Tooltip</code>, as shown in the example at the top of this page.</p>
                        <p>However, if defining the tooltip's content next to its trigger is not practical, you can use a detached trigger. This involves placing the <code>TooltipTrigger</code> outside of <code>Tooltip</code> and linking them with the same <code>handle</code>. The root gets the handle as its <code>id</code>, and the trigger gets <code>data-phem-tooltip-for</code>.</p>
                    </Example.description>
                    <TooltipDetachedDemo />
                </Example>
                <Example title="Multiple triggers" source="TooltipMultipleDemo">
                    <Example.description>
                        <p>A single tooltip can be opened by multiple trigger elements. You can achieve this by using the same <code>handle</code> for several detached triggers, or by placing multiple <code>TooltipTrigger</code> components inside a single <code>Tooltip</code>.</p>
                        <p>The tooltip can render different content depending on which trigger opened it. Give each trigger a <code>payload</code>: the <code>TooltipViewport</code> shows the payload of the active trigger.</p>
                    </Example.description>
                    <TooltipMultipleDemo />
                </Example>
                <Example title="Controlled mode with multiple triggers" source="TooltipControlledDemo">
                    <Example.description>
                        <p>You can control the tooltip's open state with the <code>open</code> and <code>triggerId</code> props of <code>Tooltip</code>: a render with new values opens the tooltip next to the trigger with that <code>id</code>, or closes it. The controller keeps the values of the browser in <code>data-phem-tooltip-open-value</code> and <code>data-phem-tooltip-trigger-id-value</code>.</p>
                        <p>Here, the button is in the root, and opens the tooltip without a request with the <code>phem-tooltip#show</code> action and a <code>triggerId</code> param.</p>
                    </Example.description>
                    <TooltipControlledDemo />
                </Example>
                <Example title="Animating the Tooltip" source="TooltipAnimatedDemo">
                    <Example.description>
                        <p>You can animate a tooltip as it moves between different trigger elements. This includes animating its position, size, and content.</p>
                        <p>To animate the tooltip's position, apply CSS transitions to the <code>top</code> and <code>left</code> properties of the <code>TooltipPopup</code>. To animate its size, transition its <code>width</code> and <code>height</code> to <code>var(--popup-width)</code> and <code>var(--popup-height)</code>.</p>
                        <p>To enable content animations, wrap the content in the <code>TooltipViewport</code> part. It gets a <code>data-activation-direction</code> attribute that indicates the new trigger's position relative to the previous one: up to two tokens, <code>left</code> or <code>right</code> and <code>up</code> or <code>down</code>. Match a single token with the <code>~=</code> attribute selector. Inside the viewport, the content is wrapped in <code>div</code>s with <code>data-current</code> (the incoming content) and <code>data-previous</code> (the outgoing content).</p>
                    </Example.description>
                    <TooltipAnimatedDemo />
                </Example>
            </Examples>
        </DemoPage>
    );
}
